Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.390
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:01.223
STEPHANIE WONG: Hey.

00:00:01.223 --> 00:00:02.910
It's Stephanie Wong,
and welcome back to

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:04.800
"What's New in
Networking," where we

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.930
cover what's new in networking.

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:10.170
Today, I'm covering the updates
for Core Infrastructure.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:12.450
There's a lot to catch up
on, so let's get into it.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.560
Right off the bat, we have
some exciting Cloud region

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:21.680
announcements.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:24.520
We've launched new Cloud
regions in Delhi, India;

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:27.640
Melbourne, Australia;
and Warsaw, Poland.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.980
Delhi and Melbourne
are the second regions

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.550
within those markets, enabling
in-country disaster recovery

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:36.100
for mission-critical
applications.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:39.130
Meanwhile, Warsaw is the
first region in Poland

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:41.620
and the seventh in Europe.

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:43.540
Designed for high
availability, regions

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:45.610
open with three
availability zones

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:47.320
to protect against
service disruptions

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.090
and offer a portfolio
of our key products,

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:53.260
including Compute Engine,
Kubernetes Engine, Cloud

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:56.260
Storage, Cloud
Spanner, and BigQuery.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:58.330
If you deploy workloads
in those regions,

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:01.390
you'll benefit from lower
latency and high availability

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:03.380
for your users.

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:05.960
We're also expanding our
Interconnect locations,

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:09.400
including Phoenix, Arizona;
Minneapolis, Minnesota;

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.040
Copenhagen, Denmark;
and Warsaw, Poland,

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:15.550
adding to over 110 locations.

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:18.280
Dedicated Interconnect provides
direct physical connection

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:22.120
between your on-premises
network and Google's network.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:23.740
Dedicated Interconnect
enables you

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:26.590
to transfer large amounts of
data between networks, which

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:30.100
can be more cost-effective than
purchasing additional bandwidth

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.380
over the public internet.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:34.150
With additional
locations, we're making

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:37.390
it more accessible for you to
get direct physical connection

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:39.430
from your own network
to Google's, including

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:41.980
up to 100 gigabits
per second circuit.

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:44.740
Next up, Cloud
Router's new features.

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:47.620
Cloud Router is our
fully-managed service that uses

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.510
Border Gateway Protocol--

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:52.750
BGP-- to advertise
IP address ranges.

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:54.160
Like you saw in
the last section,

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:56.950
it's used to setup Dedicated
Interconnect, as well

00:01:56.950 --> 00:02:00.070
as high-availability VPN,
and serves as a control

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:02.260
plane for Cloud NAT.

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:06.220
You can now disable or enable
a BGP session on Cloud Router

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:08.169
at a per-peer level.

00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:12.130
Previously, you were not able to
enable or disable specific BGP

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:14.170
sessions on Cloud Router.

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:18.370
You can now do so by issuing a
command through the API, CLI,

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:22.780
gcloud, or the Google Cloud
UI without terminating the BGP

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:23.980
session.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.960
Say you've setup
Cloud Router, but you

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:27.940
want to change the
router configuration when

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:30.130
migrating to a
different BGP peer

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:32.560
or performing
maintenance on a peer.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.870
You can disable the
session per-peer safely

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:37.060
with a quick command,
update the settings,

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:39.100
and enable the session.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.920
This gives you the flexibility
to make configuration changes

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:46.120
per-peer without resetting
the BGP session or disabling

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:49.900
and re-enabling the interconnect
or VPN link it sits on.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.720
Now let's talk about
the Keepalive timer.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:55.000
The Cloud Router and
your on-premise router

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.690
maintain communication
by using timer settings.

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:02.350
The Keepalive timer is the
interval between periodic BGP

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:05.320
heartbeat messages exchanged
between a Cloud Router

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:09.250
and the on-premises router,
and indicates whether a router

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:12.310
is reachable to its BGP peer.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:15.400
You can now modify the
Keepalive timer on Cloud Router.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:19.700
Previously, it was static, with
a default value of 20 seconds.

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:22.840
But now you can change it
to a value between 20 and 60

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:23.950
seconds.

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:25.720
This way, you can
set a higher value

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:29.110
on the Google Cloud side in case
you have a longer hold timer

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:31.480
before a failure is triggered.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:35.740
Once the hold timer is hit,
the BGP session is torn down.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:38.290
One thing to note is we
recommend setting the Keepalive

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:41.140
timers to the same value
on your on-premises router

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:43.210
and the Cloud Router.

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:44.800
Together, these
features provide you

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.900
with flexibility in
managing and troubleshooting

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:49.210
BGP sessions with Cloud Router.

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:53.110
Serverless VPC access allows you
to create VPC connectors that

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:56.050
connect serverless services,
like App Engine, Cloud

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:59.260
Run, and Cloud Functions,
to a VPC network.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:02.110
When you create a connector
in your Google Cloud project,

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:05.500
you attach it to a specific
VPC network and region.

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:07.750
You can then configure
your serverless services

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:10.570
to use the connector for
outbound network traffic,

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:12.790
like accessing Compute
Engine instances

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:16.660
and any other resources
with an internal IP address.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:19.120
While serverless VPC
access automatically

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:20.950
provisions throughput
for a connector

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:23.470
in 100 megabits per
second increments,

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:25.690
based on the amount of
traffic sent through,

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:27.370
you could still face
throughput limits

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:30.850
if the connector was deployed
using a smaller machine type.

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:33.520
With this update, you can
select larger instances

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:37.720
to support more throughput
and set new scaling settings.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:39.130
You can set the
connector machine

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:42.700
type and the min and max number
of instances it can scale to.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:44.710
If traffic requires
it, the connector

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:47.062
can scale up to the
max number configured.

00:04:47.062 --> 00:04:48.520
With the update,
you can achieve up

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:50.890
to 16 gigabits per
second of throughput

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:53.770
by specifying a
larger machine type.

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:56.740
That's it for networking
updates for Core Infrastructure.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:58.240
Check out the links
to learn more,

00:04:58.240 --> 00:04:59.980
and stay tuned for
our next episode

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:01.540
of "What's New in Networking."

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:04.890
[MUSIC PLAYING]

